# Sashimi headless-AI difficulty baseline

Date: 2026-07-03

## Question

The headless hero AI (`hero_ai`, slot 4) wins every tested seed at the
shipped tuning (`CombatConfig.damage_taken_ratio = 1.0`). At what ratio --
the fraction of incoming damage heroes take; the single difficulty knob of
the port -- does its win rate fall to 50%, and how high can the knob go
before the game stops being winnable half the time?

## Answer

**damage_taken_ratio ~ 6.1** (posterior median 6.09, mean 6.10 +/- 0.16,
95% credible interval **[5.80, 6.43]**), from 480 games over 24
evaluations. The headless AI is far more robust than the shipped tuning
assumes: it holds a >= 90% win rate up to ~3x damage and only falls to a
coin flip at ~6x.

## Method

Bayesian threshold search over full seeded games, adapted from the GP+EI
optimizer in `~/Dev/llm-dev/llm/lib/bayesian_concurrency_optimizer.py`
(same suggest/observe/converge architecture; model and acquisition replaced
because the observable here is a binomial win count and the target is a
50% *crossing*, not a maximum):

- **Model**: grid posterior over a floored logistic win curve
  `p_win(r; m, s, f) = f + (1 - f) / (1 + exp((r - m) / s))` with exact
  binomial likelihood per evaluation (the noise model). Uniform prior on
  the crossing location m and the floor f, log-uniform on the softness s.
- **Why a floor**: damage saturates once one hit one-shots a hero. Ratios
  8/12/16/24 produce bit-identical games per seed, and some seeds stay
  winnable there, so p_win tends to f > 0 as r grows. Without f the
  logistic fit is biased. The floor is only weakly identified by the
  near-crossing data the search concentrates on (posterior mean f ~ 0.21,
  broad); a direct scoping probe at ratios 8-24 saw 1/6 wins on a fixed
  seed panel, consistent with the posterior.
- **Reported threshold**: the derived 50% crossing
  `r50 = m + s * ln(0.5 / (0.5 - f))` -- posterior median with a 95%
  credible interval, not a point estimate.
- **Acquisition**: next ratio = argmin of the expected posterior variance
  of r50 over the binomial predictive distribution of the next 20-game
  batch (the threshold-search analogue of Expected Improvement).
- **Initial design**: fixed curve ratios 1.0 / 1.5 / 2.0 / 3.0 (also the
  reporting curve), then BO iterations until the CI width target or the
  game budget is reached. The search range starts at [1, 10] and expands
  if posterior mass piles up at the top edge.

### Run path (WASM in Node)

Each game runs the sashimi WASM module headless in Node
(`sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_runner.js`), the same fast path as
`tests/test_wasm.js`: a full 10800-tick game takes about a second. A fresh
module instance per game gives each game its own `FLECS_SEED` (via the
`ENV` runtime export in a `preRun` hook -- Emscripten does not inherit
`process.env`).

**How an arbitrary ratio enters** (root decisions 13/14/18): the WASM
`.data` bundle is baked at link time, so the on-disk `--overrides`
directory pattern cannot reach it. The runner instead writes a *generated
named config* (`difficulty-probe`) into the MEMFS copy of
`sandboxes/sashimi/app.yaml` before `wasm_init_with_config` -- the ratio
still enters via YAML named config through the exact same
`bridge_named_config` code path as the tracked `headless-verify` profile.
No engine or game C code changed; the only engine-surface change is
adding `ENV,FS` to the sashimi target's `EXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS`
(CMakeLists.txt), a test-only export surface. A native runner was
rejected because it would need a new executable target plus an on-disk
sandbox copy per probed ratio -- strictly more surface.

### Seeds policy

Deterministic and distinct per evaluation: evaluation `j` uses seeds
`base_seed + 1000*j + i` for `i in 0..N-1`, with `base_seed = 20260703`
pinned in the state file. Given a seed, a game is fully deterministic
(verified by the determinism sanity check), so every number below is
reproducible from the state file's seed lists.

### Sanity checks (`--sanity`, all PASS)

- The generated config reaches the damage math: on the same seed, the
  first hero hit lands on the same tick at ratio 1.0 and 2.0 (identical
  pre-damage trajectories) and its damage exactly doubles
  (13.125 -> 26.250).
- Cumulative hero damage rises with the ratio on the same seed.
- High-ratio (8.0) losses end as `Defeat - all heroes down` before the
  10800-tick victory time, never as timeouts.
- Same seed + same ratio replays to the identical outcome tick.

## Results

480 games, 24 evaluations of 20 games each (one evaluation ratio was
visited twice, 40 games), ~1.2 s per game through the WASM module.
Convergence stopped at the game budget with a 95% CI width of 0.64;
per-evaluation observed rates near the crossing scatter by the expected
binomial noise (SE ~ 0.11 at N=20), which the posterior integrates out.

**Threshold (50% win rate): median 6.09, mean 6.10 +/- 0.16 sd, 95% CI
[5.80, 6.43].**

Win-rate curve (observed = wins/games at that exact ratio; posterior =
model p_win with 95% band):

| ratio | wins/games | observed | posterior p_win |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.00 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.99 [0.98, 1.00] |
| 1.50 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.99 [0.96, 1.00] |
| 2.00 | 20/20 | 1.00 | 0.98 [0.95, 0.99] |
| 3.00 | 18/20 | 0.90 | 0.94 [0.89, 0.98] |
| 3.97 | 17/20 | 0.85 | 0.87 [0.79, 0.94] |
| 5.11 | 15/20 | 0.75 | 0.69 [0.61, 0.77] |
| 5.49 | 13/20 | 0.65 | 0.62 [0.55, 0.68] |
| 5.86-5.92 | 45/80 | 0.56 | 0.54 [0.48, 0.59] |
| 5.97-6.06 | 37/80 | 0.46 | 0.51 [0.46, 0.57] |
| 6.11-6.16 | 32/60 | 0.53 | 0.49 [0.44, 0.55] |
| 6.22-6.37 | 54/120 | 0.45 | 0.46 [0.39, 0.53] |

Posterior curve at round ratios: p(4.0) = 0.86, p(5.0) = 0.71,
p(6.0) = 0.52, p(6.1) = 0.50, p(6.5) = 0.43, p(7.0) = 0.37,
p(8.0) = 0.28.

Integrity of the 480 games: all 291 wins end at exactly tick 10800
(victory), all 189 losses end as `Defeat - all heroes down` strictly
before tick 10800 -- no timeouts, no ambiguous endings.

Raw per-game records (seed, outcome, final tick) live in
`build/difficulty-search/state.json`; the aggregate is in
`build/difficulty-search/summary.json`.

## How to rerun

```bash
./tools/build-wasm.sh sashimi        # build the module (once)
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --sanity   # checks
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --jobs 6   # search
python3 sandboxes/sashimi/tools/difficulty_search.py --report   # re-report
```

State persists to `build/difficulty-search/state.json` after every
evaluation; the search is resumable (finished evaluations are never
re-run) and `--report` regenerates the tables without running games.
Delete the state file to start a fresh search (e.g. after AI or balance
changes -- this document describes the AI as of the commit that adds it).
# GENERATED by sandboxes/sashimi/tools/generate_content.py -- do not edit by hand.
# Source: Assets/Prefabs/Abilities/AbilityBase.prefab
# (AbilityConfig + AbilityState) folded onto the hitbox
# archetype: in this port an ability IS its hitbox entity
# with timing/ready data attached, matching how Unity bakes
# the AbilitySO onto the ProximityDamageEntity child.
name: AbilityBase
abstract: true
extends: ProximityDamageEntity
components:
  AbilityConfig: { id: 0, windup: 0, active: 0, recovery: 0, cooldown: 0, start_on_cooldown: false, min_range: 0, max_range: 0 }
  AbilityState: { stage: 0, ticks_remaining: 0 }
